A||||2||sprnt96||lowering springs||||1||
Z||000000||sprnt96||08-20-2001||11:46 PM||||This may be a dumb question...but if I put lowering springs on my 84Se do I have to change anything else other than reallign?? I wasn't sure if everything else will line up and hook up the same or if I was going to have to change a bunch of other things in the front and rear end?||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||08-20-2001||11:51 PM||||Everything will just bolt up/line up, but it would be a great time to replace lower balljoints, etc.||||reg||1
Z||000002||2birds||08-21-2001||12:56 AM||||Hey, a fellow Iowan (I'm from Marion, originally).<P>If you don't have aftermarket struts with camber adjustment slots, you will have to get camber kits for the rear.  Monroe gas-matics have them, and they are cheap. KYB's probably do, too.  Good time to replace them, anyway, if you haven't yet. <BR> <BR>Moog makes slotted upper ball joints for the front end, so you can adjust the camber up there, too.  I got mine for $20 a piece from Western Auto or Pep Boys, I think (6 years ago).  <P>My experience has been that most Sears stores have the lowest alignment racks around, and they haven't charged me extra for the additional adjustment capabilities of my front end.  Never had mine at the Lindale Store, though.<P>I put polyurethane all around in mine at the same time, and used Suspension Techniques springs (linear rate), from JC Whitney.  I'd spend the extra $40 and get the Eibach springs (variable rate) if I could do it over.  Try <A HREF="http://www.shox.com" TARGET=_blank>www.shox.com</A>  (RD Enterprises) for shock/spring prices.  I got Tokico struts and shocks for my T-bird from them, and they beat everyone's price (with my TCCOA club discount).  A set of Eibach springs for the 'bird were only about $200.  Maybe they will give us a PFF club discount???<P>||||reg||1
